
This directory is intended for PlatformIO Unit Testing and project tests.

Unit Testing is a software testing method by which individual units of
source code, sets of one or more MCU program modules together with associated
control data, usage procedures, and operating procedures, are tested to
determine whether they are fit for use. Unit testing finds problems early
in the development cycle.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing:
- https://docs.platformio.org/page/plus/unit-testing.html

Standalone USB MIDI host regressions

CV output regressions (Python 3 and g++, no firmware upload):

    python3 test/cvoutput_midi_host_test.py
    python3 ../my_libraries/parameters/test_cvoutput.py

The CV MIDI test compiles production routing, pool eligibility, mode changes,
Park policy, and scene-setting callbacks against mock outputs. It checks
On/Direct/Off, active-note cleanup, dedicated-note preservation, legacy
Pooled=false compatibility, and scene round trips. The library test compiles
the real DAC conversion, MIDI/lock guards, and feedback calibration state,
including fine-step bounds, adjacent search codes, lock isolation, voltage
readout, and modulation-only zero base. These do not measure physical CVs.

From the project directory, using Node.js and g++ with UBSan support:

	node test/apc_display_host_test.js
	node test/usb_reconnect_host_test.js
	node test/menu_page_host_test.js
	node test/routing_page_host_test.js
	node test/midi_connections_host_test.js
	node test/playback_tick_host_test.js
	node test/midibass_host_test.js
	node test/actionlist_host_test.js

These compile extracted production functions with mocked USB hardware, without
running PlatformIO or installing firmware dependencies. The display/transmit
test expects USBHost_t36 at ../my_libraries/USBHost_t36; USBHOST_T36_DIR can
override that location.

The APC40 MKII adapter test also uses Node.js and g++:

	node test/apc40mkii_host_test.js

It compiles the production adapter and transport with the real APC40 library.
It checks bank/track identity, app-originated values, independent inactive banks,
automatic unipolar/bipolar rings, redundant-feedback suppression, relative Cue
deltas and clamping, fader channels, and retained state after reconnect.
APC40MKII_DIR, PARAMETERS_DIR and FOUNDLING_DIR can override the sibling library
locations. It does not verify physical LED rings or build/upload firmware.

The routing-page test compiles production behaviour-page setup, endpoint page
lookup, and popup availability checks with mock menus. It covers behaviours
that create custom pages without returning items (including Euclidian's four
source roles), standard-page precedence, explicit destinations, and no-page
behaviours. It does not build or upload firmware.
It also checks that every supported matrix target ID has a non-black colour,
including IDs beyond the original 30-colour palette.

The playback-tick test compiles the production do_tick function with mock
clock/behaviour dispatch and debug logging off. Across 768 ticks it verifies
that clocks and behaviours are still dispatched without unconditional serial
flushes. It does not measure real USB serial blocking or UI latency.

The MIDIBass test compiles the production drone/machinegun behaviour, base
Note On, and manager bar dispatch with mock transports. It checks source-first
and target-first registration, eight bars of sustain/release, machinegun modes
0..4, current/drone display state, ignored-note channels, and cleanup when an
end-bar callback is missed. It does not validate physical Neutron envelopes.

The connection test builds test/native/midi_connection_store_test.cpp with the
Arduino-free MIDIConnectionStore and policy parser/formatter. It also compiles
the production matrix public implementation, wrapper note refcount methods, and
popup actions against mock transports/menus. It covers the exact 128-route limit,
slot reuse and delivery order, disallowed routes, pause/resume note cleanup and
shared-pitch refcounts, activity timestamps, channel-policy migration, version 2
save/load round trips, version 1 and legacy loading, malformed policy logging,
and popup pause/jump/failure feedback. It also compiles the production
MIDIRoutingModel and covers its sorted per-endpoint and per-owner listings,
truncation, summaries, idempotent source/target/owner bulk pause and resume,
channel/quantise cycling in both directions, connect results, add-candidates,
disconnect-others, badge formatting, and the popup's bulk pause rows. Firmware
builds/uploads remain manual.
It also compiles the display-free routing panel lists (midi_routing_lists.h):
per-owner Inputs/Outputs layout, route rows with badges, held counts and
activity markers, compact summaries with truncation, the route editor (pause,
channel, quantise, jump, disconnect), the add-connection picker, section
pause/resume, rebuilding of the shared row buffer, and port "used by" link rows
(formatting, ordering, dimming, and jumping to the behaviour's page).

The ActionList test compiles mymenu's display-independent ActionListNavigator
(menuitems_actionlist_core.h, expected at ../my_libraries/mymenu) and covers
heading skip and wrap, empty lists, disabled rows, push/pop/back, select
results, the depth limit, cursor clamping after list changes, and scrolling.

The same runner tests the note recorder and console, including the real notes
command parser/dispatch: timestamp ages and wrap, source/target/owner filtering,
bounded history/overwrites, pairing mismatches, old/new quantisation migrations,
shared-pitch suppression and counter saturation, target-transform handoffs,
project reset/panic evidence, and ledger-full reporting. It runs both native and
mock Arduino critical-section variants; assertions check that serial output and
transport sends occur outside the diagnostic IRQ guards. These mocks do not
verify physical MIDI delivery or interrupt latency on hardware.

The reconnect test also compiles the actual main-loop USB polling wrapper,
previous-phase panel/dump, and boot-time SD snapshot functions. It checks a
simulated interrupted call, logger append/failure handling, and builds with
SD enabled and disabled. The library's tests/enumeration_host_test.js checks
retained record magic/phase validation, cache flushing, IRQ preservation, and
simulated warm-boot snapshot isolation. It also exercises real hub port-control
helpers under rejected/busy submissions and foreground retry, plus driver-owned
control halt capture at setup/data/status stages without counting optional
enumeration-string stalls. These are not physical retention or hub recovery
tests; see debug_notes.md for usb_debug_test reset/watchdog hardware checks.

The menu test expects mymenu and foundling at ../my_libraries. It uses C++11
with exceptions disabled and UBSan to check flat-list resizing,
debug-page membership, insertion failure, selection preservation, and retry.
It also compiles GateManager's production gate-menu loop, checking all labels
for two/four eight-gate banks and safe return on control allocation failure.
It checks freeExtRam's required SMalloc output pointers, sampling cache, and
failed/empty/full pool results using a host allocator stub.
MENU_TEST_SANITIZERS=address,undefined optionally enables AddressSanitizer too.
MENU_TEST_ARM_CXX can point to arm-none-eabi-g++ to additionally reproduce the
custom-linker errors from libstdc++ nothrow wrappers and std::function's empty
callback guard. It verifies that the production allocation and no-exceptions
callback overrides avoid those errors. When using a Windows compiler from WSL, set TMPDIR
to a Windows-accessible temporary directory and provide the compiler's WSL path.
Flat-list arrays use new[]/delete[] so application allocator overrides apply;
usb_teensy_clocker's override reserves internal RAM before spilling into PSRAM.

USB MIDI congestion handling

All MIDIDeviceBase send methods retain the direct transmit-buffer path when
uncongested. When buffers are busy, ordinary MIDI messages use a 32-packet FIFO.
USBHOST_MIDI_TX_QUEUE_SIZE can override that capacity via a global build flag;
it must be consistent in the library and application translation units.
MIDI realtime messages (clock, start, continue, stop, sensing, reset) use a
separate 16-packet FIFO, bypass ordinary software backlog, and immediately try
to flush partial USB packets. Each FIFO preserves its own ordering; realtime
can interleave with SysEx. Already submitted USB packets cannot be overtaken.

Completion and timer callbacks drain at most 16 messages per invocation. Sends
never spin waiting for USB progress. Default packet storage costs 192 bytes per device,
plus bookkeeping. Disconnect clears queued messages so they cannot reach a
replacement device. send_now() explicitly flushes partial packets; it does not
wait for delivery.

The smaller static ordinary queue saves 4,608 bytes of RAM1 across 12 MIDI slots
compared with the original 128-packet queues, retaining realtime capacity and
latency behavior. The host test runs both capacities in static and allocated modes.
The firmware's 8 KB error / 16 KB warning stack thresholds are unchanged; a
fresh firmware link must confirm the actual remaining stack reserve.

Optional startup-allocated queue storage

The library defaults to inline static queue arrays; it does not require PSRAM,
a custom linker, startup allocation, or allocator calls from standard users.
Define USBHOST_MIDI_TX_QUEUE_ALLOCATOR to opt into pointer-backed storage, using
the named malloc-compatible allocator. Define it globally for both the library
and application. Call beginTransmitQueues() once per device in setup(), with
interrupts enabled and memory initialized, before Usb.begin(); never call it
from an interrupt. It is idempotent and returns false on allocation failure.
Failure leaves the direct send path usable, but congested writes are counted as
drops without dereferencing missing storage. usb_tx reports queue_ready.

Each device makes one allocation for both queues and retains it for its entire
driver lifetime, including across disconnects. Sending, completion, timers, and
reconnecting do not allocate/free. Only pointers and bookkeeping remain inline.
No queued data is submitted directly to USB DMA; it is copied into the existing
USB transmit buffers, so software queues do not need DMA memory placement.

pcb_studio opts into USBHOST_MIDI_TX_QUEUE_ALLOCATOR=malloc and capacity 128.
Its linker places the normal heap in RAM2, separate from RAM1 stack, just like
the standard Teensy 4.1 linker. Queue packet storage is 576 bytes per device
(6,912 bytes total plus allocator overhead); the two pointers cost 8 bytes per
device on Teensy. This preserves the larger backlog without requiring PSRAM.
Other profiles keep the compact static default. On Teensy 4.1, extmem_malloc
can be chosen explicitly instead for PSRAM with the core's internal-heap fallback;
malloc was selected here to keep the frequently accessed queues in internal RAM.
On other targets, check heap placement before assuming it frees stack space.

Queues are finite: sustained overload or a stalled endpoint can still exhaust
them. The usb_tx console command shows per-slot pending messages, pending
realtime messages, total dropped messages, and realtime drops (a subset of total).
Drop counts persist for the slot's driver lifetime, including across reconnects.
Pending counts exclude packets already in the two hardware transmit buffers.
trySendNoteOn() reports admission failure without incrementing drop counts,
allowing the APC display to retain/coalesce pending LED state and retry.

Hardware validation: test high-tempo playback, simultaneous CC/note bursts,
APC display changes, and hub reconnects; compare usb_tx drop counters before
and after. Host tests validate logic, not physical USB delivery latency.
